{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TUo3HkK9smLXT8d1TqG7Su","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# SPEC — AI Scientist (Ralphthon Track 1)\n2\t\n3\tYou are an autonomous research agent. Your mission: produce a small, real, fully verifiable\n4\tempirical research paper. Every claim traceable, every citation real, every number computed\n5\tby code in this repository. You are re-invoked in a loop; this file is your constitution and\n6\tdoes not change during the run.\n7\t\n8\t## Topic (locked 2026-07-04, Matthew's own question; feasibility spike passed — determinism,\n9\t## speed, and capability verified on the event laptop)\n10\t\n11\t**Does more context degrade model performance? If so, when does it start, and by how much?**\n12\tMeasuring how the AMOUNT, POSITION, and QUALITY\n13\tof context change a small language model's accuracy — on experiments anyone can re-run on\n14\ta laptop. The subject under test: Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct (frozen, checksummed weights in\n15\tdata/models/), run fully offline and deterministically via llama-completion.\n16\t\n17\tResearch questions:\n18\t- RQ1 (amount): How does retrieval accuracy change as context grows (~2k → 8k tokens)?\n19\t- RQ2 (position): Does WHERE the key fact sits (start / middle / end) change accuracy —\n20\t  does the known \"lost in the middle\" effect of large API models appear in a small\n21\t  open model?\n22\t- RQ3 (quality — the novel axis): Does RELEVANT-but-redundant extra context hurt less\n23\t  than IRRELEVANT filler, or does even \"good\" context eventually degrade accuracy?\n24\t- RQ4 (mitigation): Of the cheap deterministic fixes — placing the key fact first/last,\n25\t  pruning context by simple keyword overlap — which recovers the most accuracy?\n26\t\n27\tClaims are DESCRIPTIVE of the tested model — never generalize to \"all LLMs\". Known caveats\n28\tto handle honestly: one small quantized model; synthetic haystack tasks (literal and\n29\tparaphrase probes, not natural documents); accuracy on retrieval-style questions is not\n30\tgeneral capability; CPU probe budget caps the grid size.\n31\t\n32\t**Experiment design constraints (measured 2026-07-04, 8GB arm64 laptop):**\n33\t- Probe costs MEASURED at TRUE tokenizer-verified sizes (2026-07-07, this laptop):\n34\t  ~14s at 2k, ~25s at 4k, ~76s at 8k. The FULL probe set must regenerate in ≤ 45\n35\t  minutes — do this arithmetic BEFORE running any grid. The pattern that fits: full\n36\t  resolution at 2k/4k, a SPARSE top tier (~12 probes at 8k), cheap variants\n37\t  (closed-book, pruned) at small tiers only. Do not buy an expensive tier the smaller\n38\t  tiers have already answered: if accuracy has floored by 4k, an 8k tier confirms it\n39\t  at premium prices.\n40\t- SIZE BY THE REAL TOKENIZER from the FIRST lap: never size or label context tiers\n41\t  by a chars-per-token estimate — rehearsal lost two inference batches to tiers\n42\t  labeled 2k/8k that measured 565/2073 real tokens. Verify prompt sizes with\n43\t  llama-tokenize (instant, no model inference) BEFORE running a grid; measured token\n44\t  means become the tier labels and the macros. Estimates may seed the generator;\n45\t  measurements decide the labels.\n46\t- Determinism is a hard rule: --temp 0, fixed --seed, greedy decoding, no sampling.\n47\t  Same probes, same model, same numbers — this is what makes the paper verifiable.\n48\t- Haystacks/probes are generated by seeded code in analysis/ (synthetic, reproducible).\n49\t  Design probes so literal keyword match is NOT always enough (see NoLiMa exemplar) —\n50\t  include paraphrase probes, and distractor facts that nearly match. Pilot-verified\n51\t  (2026-07-04): literal probes saturate; paraphrase probes with literal-form distractors\n52\t  produce the measurable effect (errors are distractor captures — report that rate).\n53\t- The probe runner MUST be resumable: key every probe by a stable id, write raw\n54\t  outputs one file per probe, SKIP probes whose output file already exists, and print\n55\t  progress (done/total) as it runs. Laps have a hard time cap; a killed probe run may\n56\t  only cost the unfinished probes — never a restart from zero.\n57\t- Seed ONLY with stable values (explicit integers, zlib.crc32) — NEVER Python hash(),\n58\t  which is salted per process and will fail the clean-room re-run as non-determinism.\n59\t- NEVER delete raw model outputs to pass a gate. A partial grid is legitimate\n60\t  work-in-progress: mark it incomplete in the manifest, have run_all.py aggregate\n61\t  only finished cells, and let the next lap resume it. Deleting paid-for inference to\n62\t  look finished is the expensive kind of tidy — rehearsal burned two batches\n63\t  (40+ minutes) this way in one run.\n64\t- Place distractors at positions independent of the needle position, or the position\n65\t  effect (RQ2) is confounded with distractor placement.\n66\t- POSITION MUST BE PAIRED: generate a scenario family (answer code, distractor code,\n67\t  filler, and question) without using target position in its seed, then render that same\n68\t  family with the target at start, middle, and end. Report paired changes. Rehearsal v13\n69\t  used position-specific scenarios, so its strong position gradient was descriptive but\n70\t  not a valid clause-reordering test. Do not repeat that design.\n71\t- QUALITY MUST BE MATCHED: compare relevant and irrelevant filler only within cases matched\n72\t  on measured tier, target position, and question wording. Keep the two filler groups\n73\t  balanced inside every tier used for RQ3; never pool extra high-tier relevant probes into\n74\t  only one side of the contrast.\n75\t- MITIGATION MUST BE MATCHED: compute every pruning gain over the exact scenarios having\n76\t  both full-context and pruned outputs. Report paired wins, paired losses, and the matched\n77\t  denominator. Never subtract accuracies with different denominators, as rehearsal v13 did.\n78\t- Report exact-output compliance and invalid/extra-token output rates alongside accuracy.\n79\t  The paper must name the exact GGUF filename and its SHA-256, not just the model family.\n80\t\n81\t**Stretch — agent-generated idea (ONLY after paper-v2 or later is tagged):** EITHER propose\n82\tand test ONE additional hypothesis on the same frozen model, OR generate SKILL.md — a short\n83\tpractical guide (\"how much context, where to put it, when to prune\"), every rule cited to a\n84\tresults.json value. State the choice in TODO.md first; add to the paper only if every gate\n85\tpasses and the reviewer keeps or raises the score.\n86\t\n87\t**Data acquisition:** model weights are frozen during prep week (data/models/, sha256 in\n88\tCHECKSUMS.txt, re-fetchable via FETCH.sh). The agent never downloads anything; it starts\n89\tfrom the frozen weights and writes seeded generators for all probe data.\n90\t\n91\t**Fallback (switch ONLY if TODO.md contains a harness-written directive to switch):**\n92\t\"Do your high school grades predict your net worth at 30, beyond where your family\n93\tstarted?\" — NLSY97 frozen extract in data/cache/nlsy97/ (fully prepped, three rehearsal\n94\tpapers produced; exemplar wing in data/exemplars/). Battle-tested but not ML-topical.\n95\t\n96\t## Data rules (apply to any topic)\n97\t\n98\t- Public, keyless sources only. Cache every downloaded response to `data/cache/` — the loop\n99\t  must never re-fetch what it already has.\n100\t- Semantic Scholar Graph API / Crossref for citation verification (both keyless).\n101\t- NEVER fabricate, extrapolate, or hand-copy data values. If data cannot be fetched, record the blocker in TODO.md.\n102\t\n103\t## Toolchain (you are OFFLINE — the harness handles all network)\n104\t\n105\t- Python: `.venv/bin/python` — pandas, scipy, statsmodels, requests preinstalled (pinned\n106\t  in requirements.txt). Never pip install; there is no network in your sandbox.\n107\t- The model runner: `llama-completion -m data/models/qwen2.5-0.5b-instruct-q8_0.gguf\n108\t  -f <promptfile> -n <max_tokens> --temp 0 --seed 42 -c 9216 --no-display-prompt\n109\t  --simple-io` (llama.cpp; NOTE: llama-cli is interactive-only in this build — never\n110\t  use it in scripts). Call it via subprocess from analysis scripts.\n111\t- TWO-TIER PIPELINE (inference is expensive; aggregation is cheap):\n112\t  `make probes` runs `analysis/run_probes.py` (you create it) — generates all prompt\n113\t  files from seeded code, runs the model on each, writes raw outputs to `data/probes/`.\n114\t  Run it whenever you change the experiment grid. `make results` runs\n115\t  `analysis/run_all.py` — reads `data/probes/`, scores and aggregates into results.json\n116\t  (contract: analysis/RESULTS_SCHEMA.md). It must be FAST (< 1 min) and deterministic:\n117\t  the pipeline-fresh gate re-runs it every lap. `make values` regenerates\n118\t  paper/values.tex. `make paper` compiles the ICML PDF via tectonic.\n119\t  `make clean-deep && make all` is the full clean-room proof (re-runs all inference,\n120\t  ≤ 45 min) — the harness runs it before final submission; hand-edited probe outputs\n121\t  will be exposed there and in the public git history.\n122\t- Citations: you propose entries in paper/refs.bib; the HARNESS verifies them against\n123\t  Crossref/Semantic Scholar after you exit. Already-verified entries are cached in\n124\t  data/cache/citations/. Do not invent papers — unresolvable entries fail the gate.\n125\t- The frozen model: data/models/ (CHECKSUMS.txt). The literature library:\n126\t  data/exemplars/ — metadata JSON + full texts; the context-degradation wing is your\n127\t  Related Work seed. READ the full texts before writing Related Work.\n128\t\n129\t## Hard rules (gates will enforce these — violations waste an iteration)\n130\t\n131\t1. Every number, percentage, table cell, and figure in the paper comes from `results.json`,\n132\t   written only by scripts in `analysis/`. Prose uses macros from `paper/values.tex`\n133\t   (generated by `analysis/make_values.py`). Never type a numeric literal in `paper/*.tex`\n134\t   except a publication year. Use protected `paper/model.tex` macros for the model identity;\n135\t   put other fixed configuration numbers in results.json too.\n136\t2. Every entry in `paper/refs.bib` must be a real paper that resolves via Crossref or\n137\t   Semantic Scholar (title + first author match). No citation without having a resolvable record.\n138\t3. The paper must compile via `make paper` (tectonic, ICML 2026 template in `paper/`).\n139\t4. The full pipeline must re-run clean: `make clean && make all` regenerates results.json\n140\t   and the PDF from raw cached data.\n141\t5. The frozen model and fallback dataset must pass their committed SHA-256 manifests.\n142\t6. All work is committed; the repo will be public.\n143\t\n144\t## Paper requirements\n145\t\n146\t- ICML 2026 LaTeX template in anonymous submission mode. The main body is 2–4 pages\n147\t  with a hard four-page maximum; references and appendices are excluded from that count.\n148\t  Keep `\\label{main-body-end}` immediately before them so the gate measures the correct\n149\t  boundary. The PDF, a concrete title, and a self-contained abstract are the primary\n150\t  submission artifacts; code, logs, and W&B are optional and must never delay the paper.\n151\t  The short-paper format (density is not the goal, selection is) contains: Abstract, Introduction, Related\n152\t  Work (brief), Method, Results\n153\t  (≥2 figures or tables), Limitations, Reproducibility Statement.\n154\t- Honest framing: this is a small-scale replication (lost-in-the-middle, input-length\n155\t  degradation — see exemplars) + extension (the context-QUALITY axis, RQ3, and the\n156\t  mitigation comparison, RQ4, on a laptop-scale open model). Say so. Overclaiming will\n157\t  be rejected by the adversarial reviewer.\n158\t- MOTIVATING EXAMPLE: frame the paper with ONE restrained plain-language example —\n159\t  asking a question about a single clause buried in a long contract. Introduce it in\n160\t  the introduction, use it later only when it clarifies a result, and state explicitly that\n161\t  the probes are a synthetic, gradeable version of that situation — never imply real\n162\t  contracts were tested. One example used consistently; do not scatter new analogies.\n163\t- THE OPENING IS A SCENE, NOT A SUMMARY: the introduction's first paragraph must\n164\t  describe a moment the reader has lived, in second person if that helps (\"You paste\n165\t  a 50-page contract into a chatbot and ask...\"), with concrete specifics (a page\n166\t  number, a question in quotes) and ZERO terminology. Every actor (the look-alike\n167\t  decoy sentence, the model's habit of word-matching) is SHOWN in the scene before\n168\t  any section names or defines it. Test: a reader who has only used a chatbot once\n169\t  should recognize the situation by sentence two. This standard applies to the\n170\t  opening. End the scene after at most two sentences; methods and results use a conventional\n171\t  professional research register.\n172\t- PROSE STYLE (gate-enforced by harness/gates/check_style.py — violations fail the\n173\t  lap): NO em dashes of any kind (no ---, no U+2014/U+2013; rewrite with a comma,\n174\t  period, or parentheses; ASCII -- only inside numeric ranges like 1980--84), and\n175\t  NONE of the banned filler vocabulary (delve, leverage, crucial, pivotal, moreover,\n176\t  furthermore, comprehensive, landscape, showcase, foster, tapestry, paradigm,\n177\t  \"it is important to note\", and similar — the gate prints the full list on failure).\n178\t  Plain words beat impressive ones. Professional tone comes from precision, not\n179\t  vocabulary.\n180\t- PROFESSIONAL REGISTER: write for a technically literate reader outside this narrow\n181\t  subfield, not for a child and not for a marketing audience. Use normal research words\n182\t  when they are the exact words: estimate, condition, paired comparison, synthetic probe,\n183\t  confidence interval. Define specialized ideas once; do not define ordinary words such as\n184\t  baseline, mitigation, document, or accuracy. Prefer \"synthetic\" to \"made-up,\" \"estimate\"\n185\t  to \"readout,\" and \"experimental factor\" to \"knob.\" First-person plural is allowed and\n186\t  often cleaner than repeating \"this paper.\" Never narrate the writing process.\n187\t\n188\t  BAD:  \"The target clause is the clause with the correct answer. A mitigation is a cheap\n189\t         change meant to reduce errors.\"\n190\t  GOOD: \"Each document contains a vendor clause with the correct code and a buyer clause\n191\t         with a competing code. We evaluate keyword pruning as a retrieval baseline.\"\n192\t\n193\t  BAD:  \"Table 3 shows the strongest pattern in the current grid. In the contract example,\n194\t         the model is most trustworthy when the vendor clause appears first.\"\n195\t  GOOD: \"Accuracy fell from \\accStart\\% to \\accEnd\\% when the same clause moved from the\n196\t         start to the end of the document (Table~\\ref{tab:position}).\"\n197\t\n198\t  Paragraphs should lead with the claim, then evidence, then one necessary qualification.\n199\t  Do not alternate every result with a disclaimer; collect secondary caveats in Limitations.\n200\t  Avoid three short declarative sentences in a row. Vary sentence openings naturally rather\n201\t  than replacing one repeated template with another.\n202\t- SENTENCE LAW (the most important paper standard — a paper nobody can follow is a\n203\t  failed paper regardless of its numbers). Every sentence must survive four tests,\n204\t  each shown with a real failure from rehearsal and its fix:\n205\t  1. SIMPLE + EXACT: plain words making a precise claim. If you cannot say exactly\n206\t     what a sentence claims, it is broken no matter how simple it sounds.\n207\t     BAD:  \"Putting more text in a prompt changes more than the amount a model must\n208\t           read.\" (changes more WHAT? the comparison never lands)\n209\t     GOOD: \"Increasing document length can also change the answer's relative position and\n210\t           the number of competing clauses, so an unmatched length comparison does not\n211\t           isolate a single cause.\"\n212\t  2. SUBJECTS DO THEIR VERBS: the subject of each sentence must be able to actually\n213\t     perform its verb; otherwise the sentence is making something up.\n214\t     BAD:  \"A longer prompt never changes length alone.\" (a prompt cannot change\n215\t           length — being longer IS the length change)\n216\t     GOOD: \"We move the same target clause across three positions while holding the\n217\t           question, filler, and competing clause fixed.\"\n218\t  3. NOTHING WALKS ON STAGE UNANNOUNCED: no term, name, or shorthand before the\n219\t     thing it names has been described in plain words.\n220\t     BAD:  \"The safe answer should be the clause that governs the vendor.\" (\"safe\n221\t           answer\" never defined; vendor appears with no setup)\n222\t     GOOD: introduce the situation first (\"The contract also contains a\n223\t           similar-looking clause for the buyer\"), then the term may be used.\n224\t  4. EVERY KNOB NAMES ITS MACHINE: never bare \"length, position, and filler type\" —\n225\t     always length OF WHAT, with real (macro) values.\n226\t     BAD:  \"The grid varies length, position, and filler type.\"\n227\t     GOOD: \"We vary three properties of each synthetic document: tokenizer-measured length,\n228\t           target-clause position, and whether surrounding clauses are topically related.\"\n229\t  Flow: one idea per sentence; a reader meets each concept before it is used; short\n230\t  sentences mixed with medium ones. Read every paragraph as if aloud — a stumble is\n231\t  a defect to fix, not a style choice.\n232\t- THE QUESTIONS ARE VISIBLE: end the Introduction with the paper's questions as a\n233\t  short numbered list, in the paper's own plain voice (no \"RQ\" labels, no spec\n234\t  wording), scoped to what was actually measured (use measured macros, not nominal\n235\t  sizes) — e.g. \"1. As the document grows, how much does accuracy fall?\" The Results\n236\t  section answers each question by number, with a measured value in the answering\n237\t  sentence. A reviewer must find any question and its answer in under a minute.\n238\t- READABILITY BAR: a technically literate reader who does not study long-context evaluation\n239\t  must follow the argument on one pass. Define specialized statistical terms at first use\n240\t  in one precise clause, but assume ordinary research vocabulary. Interpret every table in\n241\t  prose without mechanically starting \"Table X shows\" or repeating the contract example.\n242\t  The abstract's last sentence states the takeaway directly. Unexplained jargon is a defect;\n243\t  over-explaining ordinary words is also a defect.\n244\t- Limitations section must name at least: one small quantized model (findings may not\n245\t  transfer to larger models); synthetic probe tasks, not natural documents; retrieval\n246\t  accuracy is not general capability; grid size capped by the CPU probe budget (state\n247\t  the budget); greedy decoding only (no sampling variance studied).\n248\t- Final section before References: a short **Reproducibility** paragraph (3–5 sentences)\n249\t  stating that every number in the paper is compiled from analysis scripts in this\n250\t  repository, that the data is frozen and checksummed in data/cache/, and that running\n251\t  `make all` regenerates every result from the raw data. Reviewers may never see a live\n252\t  demo — the paper itself must carry the proof that nothing in it can be faked.\n253\t\n254\t## Exemplar papers (anchors for tone/structure — full texts cached in data/exemplars/;\n255\t## read them, cite them, do not trust memory)\n256\t\n257\tTopic-adjacent (also the seed of Related Work — all with cached full text):\n258\t\n259\t1. Liu et al., 2023 — \"Lost in the Middle: How Language Models Use Long Contexts\"\n260\t   ← the core prior work: position effects in large API models. Our extension: does it\n261\t   hold in a laptop-scale open model, and how does context QUALITY interact?\n262\t2. Levy et al., 2024 — \"Same Task, More Tokens\" ← length alone degrades reasoning.\n263\t3. Shi et al., 2023 — \"Large Language Models Can Be Easily Distracted by Irrelevant\n264\t   Context\" (ICML) ← the quality axis's closest prior; ours varies relevant vs\n265\t   irrelevant filler systematically at small scale.\n266\t4. Hsieh et al., 2024 — \"RULER\" ← usable context is shorter than the advertised window.\n267\t5. Modarressi et al., 2025 — \"NoLiMa\" ← why literal-match needles overestimate ability;\n268\t   design probes accordingly.\n269\t6. Qwen Team, 2024 — \"Qwen2.5 Technical Report\" ← the subject model; cite for specs.\n270\t\n271\tStyle/structure models (short, clean empirical papers in ICML format conventions):\n272\t\n273\t7. Schaeffer et al., 2023 — \"Are Emergent Abilities of Large Language Models a Mirage?\"\n274\t   ← the shape to imitate: skeptical question → simple measurements → honest answer.\n275\t8. Dodge et al., 2019 — \"Show Your Work\" ← reporting norms; cite in Reproducibility.\n276\t\n277\t## Rubric (the adversarial reviewer's RUBRIC score /10 follows the competition's\n278\t## ICML-style form; the ratchet requires rubric ≥ 6, confirmed by a second read)\n279\t\n280\t- Soundness: method faithfully replicates the cited approach; statistics appropriate;\n281\t  paired/matched comparisons are valid; probe counts and uncertainty accompany estimates;\n282\t  limitations are real and claims are sized to evidence.\n283\t- Presentation: a reviewer can find the question, method, contribution, and headline result\n284\t  in 60 seconds; prose is professional; displays are selective and legible.\n285\t- Significance: the paper explains who should care, what decision the evidence informs, and\n286\t  why the result matters beyond this one synthetic grid without overstating generality.\n287\t- Originality: the quality axis, matched mitigation comparison, laptop-scale open-model\n288\t  evidence, or an agent-generated finding adds a clear distinction from the cited work.\n289\t- Reproducibility supports soundness: a stranger can trace and rerun every reported result.\n290\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"57f12af0-c867-4e68-a3f6-04a967999e3f","uuid":"86f95c6f-f83c-47c5-9f58-37b1d82ed205","timestamp":"2026-07-12T04:32:51.236Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/matthewchan/ralphm/Ralphm/SPEC.md","content":"# SPEC — AI Scientist (Ralphthon Track 1)\n\nYou are an autonomous research agent. Your mission: produce a small, real, fully verifiable\nempirical research paper. Every claim traceable, every citation real, every number computed\nby code in this repository. You are re-invoked in a loop; this file is your constitution and\ndoes not change during the run.\n\n## Topic (locked 2026-07-04, Matthew's own question; feasibility spike passed — determinism,\n## speed, and capability verified on the event laptop)\n\n**Does more context degrade model performance? If so, when does it start, and by how much?**\nMeasuring how the AMOUNT, POSITION, and QUALITY\nof context change a small language model's accuracy — on experiments anyone can re-run on\na laptop. The subject under test: Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct (frozen, checksummed weights in\ndata/models/), run fully offline and deterministically via llama-completion.\n\nResearch questions:\n- RQ1 (amount): How does retrieval accuracy change as context grows (~2k → 8k tokens)?\n- RQ2 (position): Does WHERE the key fact sits (start / middle / end) change accuracy —\n  does the known \"lost in the middle\" effect of large API models appear in a small\n  open model?\n- RQ3 (quality — the novel axis): Does RELEVANT-but-redundant extra context hurt less\n  than IRRELEVANT filler, or does even \"good\" context eventually degrade accuracy?\n- RQ4 (mitigation): Of the cheap deterministic fixes — placing the key fact first/last,\n  pruning context by simple keyword overlap — which recovers the most accuracy?\n\nClaims are DESCRIPTIVE of the tested model — never generalize to \"all LLMs\". Known caveats\nto handle honestly: one small quantized model; synthetic haystack tasks (literal and\nparaphrase probes, not natural documents); accuracy on retrieval-style questions is not\ngeneral capability; CPU probe budget caps the grid size.\n\n**Experiment design constraints (measured 2026-07-04, 8GB arm64 laptop):**\n- Probe costs MEASURED at TRUE tokenizer-verified sizes (2026-07-07, this laptop):\n  ~14s at 2k, ~25s at 4k, ~76s at 8k. The FULL probe set must regenerate in ≤ 45\n  minutes — do this arithmetic BEFORE running any grid. The pattern that fits: full\n  resolution at 2k/4k, a SPARSE top tier (~12 probes at 8k), cheap variants\n  (closed-book, pruned) at small tiers only. Do not buy an expensive tier the smaller\n  tiers have already answered: if accuracy has floored by 4k, an 8k tier confirms it\n  at premium prices.\n- SIZE BY THE REAL TOKENIZER from the FIRST lap: never size or label context tiers\n  by a chars-per-token estimate — rehearsal lost two inference batches to tiers\n  labeled 2k/8k that measured 565/2073 real tokens. Verify prompt sizes with\n  llama-tokenize (instant, no model inference) BEFORE running a grid; measured token\n  means become the tier labels and the macros. Estimates may seed the generator;\n  measurements decide the labels.\n- Determinism is a hard rule: --temp 0, fixed --seed, greedy decoding, no sampling.\n  Same probes, same model, same numbers — this is what makes the paper verifiable.\n- Haystacks/probes are generated by seeded code in analysis/ (synthetic, reproducible).\n  Design probes so literal keyword match is NOT always enough (see NoLiMa exemplar) —\n  include paraphrase probes, and distractor facts that nearly match. Pilot-verified\n  (2026-07-04): literal probes saturate; paraphrase probes with literal-form distractors\n  produce the measurable effect (errors are distractor captures — report that rate).\n- The probe runner MUST be resumable: key every probe by a stable id, write raw\n  outputs one file per probe, SKIP probes whose output file already exists, and print\n  progress (done/total) as it runs. Laps have a hard time cap; a killed probe run may\n  only cost the unfinished probes — never a restart from zero.\n- Seed ONLY with stable values (explicit integers, zlib.crc32) — NEVER Python hash(),\n  which is salted per process and will fail the clean-room re-run as non-determinism.\n- NEVER delete raw model outputs to pass a gate. A partial grid is legitimate\n  work-in-progress: mark it incomplete in the manifest, have run_all.py aggregate\n  only finished cells, and let the next lap resume it. Deleting paid-for inference to\n  look finished is the expensive kind of tidy — rehearsal burned two batches\n  (40+ minutes) this way in one run.\n- Place distractors at positions independent of the needle position, or the position\n  effect (RQ2) is confounded with distractor placement.\n- POSITION MUST BE PAIRED: generate a scenario family (answer code, distractor code,\n  filler, and question) without using target position in its seed, then render that same\n  family with the target at start, middle, and end. Report paired changes. Rehearsal v13\n  used position-specific scenarios, so its strong position gradient was descriptive but\n  not a valid clause-reordering test. Do not repeat that design.\n- QUALITY MUST BE MATCHED: compare relevant and irrelevant filler only within cases matched\n  on measured tier, target position, and question wording. Keep the two filler groups\n  balanced inside every tier used for RQ3; never pool extra high-tier relevant probes into\n  only one side of the contrast.\n- MITIGATION MUST BE MATCHED: compute every pruning gain over the exact scenarios having\n  both full-context and pruned outputs. Report paired wins, paired losses, and the matched\n  denominator. Never subtract accuracies with different denominators, as rehearsal v13 did.\n- Report exact-output compliance and invalid/extra-token output rates alongside accuracy.\n  The paper must name the exact GGUF filename and its SHA-256, not just the model family.\n\n**Stretch — agent-generated idea (ONLY after paper-v2 or later is tagged):** EITHER propose\nand test ONE additional hypothesis on the same frozen model, OR generate SKILL.md — a short\npractical guide (\"how much context, where to put it, when to prune\"), every rule cited to a\nresults.json value. State the choice in TODO.md first; add to the paper only if every gate\npasses and the reviewer keeps or raises the score.\n\n**Data acquisition:** model weights are frozen during prep week (data/models/, sha256 in\nCHECKSUMS.txt, re-fetchable via FETCH.sh). The agent never downloads anything; it starts\nfrom the frozen weights and writes seeded generators for all probe data.\n\n**Fallback (switch ONLY if TODO.md contains a harness-written directive to switch):**\n\"Do your high school grades predict your net worth at 30, beyond where your family\nstarted?\" — NLSY97 frozen extract in data/cache/nlsy97/ (fully prepped, three rehearsal\npapers produced; exemplar wing in data/exemplars/). Battle-tested but not ML-topical.\n\n## Data rules (apply to any topic)\n\n- Public, keyless sources only. Cache every downloaded response to `data/cache/` — the loop\n  must never re-fetch what it already has.\n- Semantic Scholar Graph API / Crossref for citation verification (both keyless).\n- NEVER fabricate, extrapolate, or hand-copy data values. If data cannot be fetched, record the blocker in TODO.md.\n\n## Toolchain (you are OFFLINE — the harness handles all network)\n\n- Python: `.venv/bin/python` — pandas, scipy, statsmodels, requests preinstalled (pinned\n  in requirements.txt). Never pip install; there is no network in your sandbox.\n- The model runner: `llama-completion -m data/models/qwen2.5-0.5b-instruct-q8_0.gguf\n  -f <promptfile> -n <max_tokens> --temp 0 --seed 42 -c 9216 --no-display-prompt\n  --simple-io` (llama.cpp; NOTE: llama-cli is interactive-only in this build — never\n  use it in scripts). Call it via subprocess from analysis scripts.\n- TWO-TIER PIPELINE (inference is expensive; aggregation is cheap):\n  `make probes` runs `analysis/run_probes.py` (you create it) — generates all prompt\n  files from seeded code, runs the model on each, writes raw outputs to `data/probes/`.\n  Run it whenever you change the experiment grid. `make results` runs\n  `analysis/run_all.py` — reads `data/probes/`, scores and aggregates into results.json\n  (contract: analysis/RESULTS_SCHEMA.md). It must be FAST (< 1 min) and deterministic:\n  the pipeline-fresh gate re-runs it every lap. `make values` regenerates\n  paper/values.tex. `make paper` compiles the ICML PDF via tectonic.\n  `make clean-deep && make all` is the full clean-room proof (re-runs all inference,\n  ≤ 45 min) — the harness runs it before final submission; hand-edited probe outputs\n  will be exposed there and in the public git history.\n- Citations: you propose entries in paper/refs.bib; the HARNESS verifies them against\n  Crossref/Semantic Scholar after you exit. Already-verified entries are cached in\n  data/cache/citations/. Do not invent papers — unresolvable entries fail the gate.\n- The frozen model: data/models/ (CHECKSUMS.txt). The literature library:\n  data/exemplars/ — metadata JSON + full texts; the context-degradation wing is your\n  Related Work seed. READ the full texts before writing Related Work.\n\n## Hard rules (gates will enforce these — violations waste an iteration)\n\n1. Every number, percentage, table cell, and figure in the paper comes from `results.json`,\n   written only by scripts in `analysis/`. Prose uses macros from `paper/values.tex`\n   (generated by `analysis/make_values.py`). Never type a numeric literal in `paper/*.tex`\n   except a publication year. Use protected `paper/model.tex` macros for the model identity;\n   put other fixed configuration numbers in results.json too.\n2. Every entry in `paper/refs.bib` must be a real paper that resolves via Crossref or\n   Semantic Scholar (title + first author match). No citation without having a resolvable record.\n3. The paper must compile via `make paper` (tectonic, ICML 2026 template in `paper/`).\n4. The full pipeline must re-run clean: `make clean && make all` regenerates results.json\n   and the PDF from raw cached data.\n5. The frozen model and fallback dataset must pass their committed SHA-256 manifests.\n6. All work is committed; the repo will be public.\n\n## Paper requirements\n\n- ICML 2026 LaTeX template in anonymous submission mode. The main body is 2–4 pages\n  with a hard four-page maximum; references and appendices are excluded from that count.\n  Keep `\\label{main-body-end}` immediately before them so the gate measures the correct\n  boundary. The PDF, a concrete title, and a self-contained abstract are the primary\n  submission artifacts; code, logs, and W&B are optional and must never delay the paper.\n  The short-paper format (density is not the goal, selection is) contains: Abstract, Introduction, Related\n  Work (brief), Method, Results\n  (≥2 figures or tables), Limitations, Reproducibility Statement.\n- Honest framing: this is a small-scale replication (lost-in-the-middle, input-length\n  degradation — see exemplars) + extension (the context-QUALITY axis, RQ3, and the\n  mitigation comparison, RQ4, on a laptop-scale open model). Say so. Overclaiming will\n  be rejected by the adversarial reviewer.\n- MOTIVATING EXAMPLE: frame the paper with ONE restrained plain-language example —\n  asking a question about a single clause buried in a long contract. Introduce it in\n  the introduction, use it later only when it clarifies a result, and state explicitly that\n  the probes are a synthetic, gradeable version of that situation — never imply real\n  contracts were tested. One example used consistently; do not scatter new analogies.\n- THE OPENING IS A SCENE, NOT A SUMMARY: the introduction's first paragraph must\n  describe a moment the reader has lived, in second person if that helps (\"You paste\n  a 50-page contract into a chatbot and ask...\"), with concrete specifics (a page\n  number, a question in quotes) and ZERO terminology. Every actor (the look-alike\n  decoy sentence, the model's habit of word-matching) is SHOWN in the scene before\n  any section names or defines it. Test: a reader who has only used a chatbot once\n  should recognize the situation by sentence two. This standard applies to the\n  opening. End the scene after at most two sentences; methods and results use a conventional\n  professional research register.\n- PROSE STYLE (gate-enforced by harness/gates/check_style.py — violations fail the\n  lap): NO em dashes of any kind (no ---, no U+2014/U+2013; rewrite with a comma,\n  period, or parentheses; ASCII -- only inside numeric ranges like 1980--84), and\n  NONE of the banned filler vocabulary (delve, leverage, crucial, pivotal, moreover,\n  furthermore, comprehensive, landscape, showcase, foster, tapestry, paradigm,\n  \"it is important to note\", and similar — the gate prints the full list on failure).\n  Plain words beat impressive ones. Professional tone comes from precision, not\n  vocabulary.\n- PROFESSIONAL REGISTER: write for a technically literate reader outside this narrow\n  subfield, not for a child and not for a marketing audience. Use normal research words\n  when they are the exact words: estimate, condition, paired comparison, synthetic probe,\n  confidence interval. Define specialized ideas once; do not define ordinary words such as\n  baseline, mitigation, document, or accuracy. Prefer \"synthetic\" to \"made-up,\" \"estimate\"\n  to \"readout,\" and \"experimental factor\" to \"knob.\" First-person plural is allowed and\n  often cleaner than repeating \"this paper.\" Never narrate the writing process.\n\n  BAD:  \"The target clause is the clause with the correct answer. A mitigation is a cheap\n         change meant to reduce errors.\"\n  GOOD: \"Each document contains a vendor clause with the correct code and a buyer clause\n         with a competing code. We evaluate keyword pruning as a retrieval baseline.\"\n\n  BAD:  \"Table 3 shows the strongest pattern in the current grid. In the contract example,\n         the model is most trustworthy when the vendor clause appears first.\"\n  GOOD: \"Accuracy fell from \\accStart\\% to \\accEnd\\% when the same clause moved from the\n         start to the end of the document (Table~\\ref{tab:position}).\"\n\n  Paragraphs should lead with the claim, then evidence, then one necessary qualification.\n  Do not alternate every result with a disclaimer; collect secondary caveats in Limitations.\n  Avoid three short declarative sentences in a row. Vary sentence openings naturally rather\n  than replacing one repeated template with another.\n- SENTENCE LAW (the most important paper standard — a paper nobody can follow is a\n  failed paper regardless of its numbers). Every sentence must survive four tests,\n  each shown with a real failure from rehearsal and its fix:\n  1. SIMPLE + EXACT: plain words making a precise claim. If you cannot say exactly\n     what a sentence claims, it is broken no matter how simple it sounds.\n     BAD:  \"Putting more text in a prompt changes more than the amount a model must\n           read.\" (changes more WHAT? the comparison never lands)\n     GOOD: \"Increasing document length can also change the answer's relative position and\n           the number of competing clauses, so an unmatched length comparison does not\n           isolate a single cause.\"\n  2. SUBJECTS DO THEIR VERBS: the subject of each sentence must be able to actually\n     perform its verb; otherwise the sentence is making something up.\n     BAD:  \"A longer prompt never changes length alone.\" (a prompt cannot change\n           length — being longer IS the length change)\n     GOOD: \"We move the same target clause across three positions while holding the\n           question, filler, and competing clause fixed.\"\n  3. NOTHING WALKS ON STAGE UNANNOUNCED: no term, name, or shorthand before the\n     thing it names has been described in plain words.\n     BAD:  \"The safe answer should be the clause that governs the vendor.\" (\"safe\n           answer\" never defined; vendor appears with no setup)\n     GOOD: introduce the situation first (\"The contract also contains a\n           similar-looking clause for the buyer\"), then the term may be used.\n  4. EVERY KNOB NAMES ITS MACHINE: never bare \"length, position, and filler type\" —\n     always length OF WHAT, with real (macro) values.\n     BAD:  \"The grid varies length, position, and filler type.\"\n     GOOD: \"We vary three properties of each synthetic document: tokenizer-measured length,\n           target-clause position, and whether surrounding clauses are topically related.\"\n  Flow: one idea per sentence; a reader meets each concept before it is used; short\n  sentences mixed with medium ones. Read every paragraph as if aloud — a stumble is\n  a defect to fix, not a style choice.\n- THE QUESTIONS ARE VISIBLE: end the Introduction with the paper's questions as a\n  short numbered list, in the paper's own plain voice (no \"RQ\" labels, no spec\n  wording), scoped to what was actually measured (use measured macros, not nominal\n  sizes) — e.g. \"1. As the document grows, how much does accuracy fall?\" The Results\n  section answers each question by number, with a measured value in the answering\n  sentence. A reviewer must find any question and its answer in under a minute.\n- READABILITY BAR: a technically literate reader who does not study long-context evaluation\n  must follow the argument on one pass. Define specialized statistical terms at first use\n  in one precise clause, but assume ordinary research vocabulary. Interpret every table in\n  prose without mechanically starting \"Table X shows\" or repeating the contract example.\n  The abstract's last sentence states the takeaway directly. Unexplained jargon is a defect;\n  over-explaining ordinary words is also a defect.\n- Limitations section must name at least: one small quantized model (findings may not\n  transfer to larger models); synthetic probe tasks, not natural documents; retrieval\n  accuracy is not general capability; grid size capped by the CPU probe budget (state\n  the budget); greedy decoding only (no sampling variance studied).\n- Final section before References: a short **Reproducibility** paragraph (3–5 sentences)\n  stating that every number in the paper is compiled from analysis scripts in this\n  repository, that the data is frozen and checksummed in data/cache/, and that running\n  `make all` regenerates every result from the raw data. Reviewers may never see a live\n  demo — the paper itself must carry the proof that nothing in it can be faked.\n\n## Exemplar papers (anchors for tone/structure — full texts cached in data/exemplars/;\n## read them, cite them, do not trust memory)\n\nTopic-adjacent (also the seed of Related Work — all with cached full text):\n\n1. Liu et al., 2023 — \"Lost in the Middle: How Language Models Use Long Contexts\"\n   ← the core prior work: position effects in large API models. Our extension: does it\n   hold in a laptop-scale open model, and how does context QUALITY interact?\n2. Levy et al., 2024 — \"Same Task, More Tokens\" ← length alone degrades reasoning.\n3. Shi et al., 2023 — \"Large Language Models Can Be Easily Distracted by Irrelevant\n   Context\" (ICML) ← the quality axis's closest prior; ours varies relevant vs\n   irrelevant filler systematically at small scale.\n4. Hsieh et al., 2024 — \"RULER\" ← usable context is shorter than the advertised window.\n5. Modarressi et al., 2025 — \"NoLiMa\" ← why literal-match needles overestimate ability;\n   design probes accordingly.\n6. Qwen Team, 2024 — \"Qwen2.5 Technical Report\" ← the subject model; cite for specs.\n\nStyle/structure models (short, clean empirical papers in ICML format conventions):\n\n7. Schaeffer et al., 2023 — \"Are Emergent Abilities of Large Language Models a Mirage?\"\n   ← the shape to imitate: skeptical question → simple measurements → honest answer.\n8. Dodge et al., 2019 — \"Show Your Work\" ← reporting norms; cite in Reproducibility.\n\n## Rubric (the adversarial reviewer's RUBRIC score /10 follows the competition's\n## ICML-style form; the ratchet requires rubric ≥ 6, confirmed by a second read)\n\n- Soundness: method faithfully replicates the cited approach; statistics appropriate;\n  paired/matched comparisons are valid; probe counts and uncertainty accompany estimates;\n  limitations are real and claims are sized to evidence.\n- Presentation: a reviewer can find the question, method, contribution, and headline result\n  in 60 seconds; prose is professional; displays are selective and legible.\n- Significance: the paper explains who should care, what decision the evidence informs, and\n  why the result matters beyond this one synthetic grid without overstating generality.\n- Originality: the quality axis, matched mitigation comparison, laptop-scale open-model\n  evidence, or an agent-generated finding adds a clear distinction from the cited work.\n- Reproducibility supports soundness: a stranger can trace and rerun every reported result.\n","numLines":290,"startLine":1,"totalLines":290}}}
